{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"The re-review BLOCKed on one T8 finding, and it is correct about the plan — but the plan\nonly, not the code.\n\nThe finding: the Approach specified the rendered candidate line as `alias -- title\n(status)`, while the retrieval the plan itself cites produces no per-candidate status.\nVerified against current `main`: `similar/lexical.py:101` is the index row\n`SELECT ticket_id, alias, title, bm25(...)`, which has no workflow-status column, and\n`:110-117` builds the returned dict with exactly `id`/`alias`/`title`/`score`. Both\ncitations are exact. An executor following that sentence literally would have had no\ndefined value to render.\n\nWhat actually shipped already resolves it, in precisely the way the reviewer suggested.\n`overlap_context_block` in the merged `overlap.py` renders\n`  - {ident} -- {title} (similarity {score})`, falling back from `alias` to `id` for\nidentity and to `?` on a non-numeric score, and its docstring states the reason: only the\nkeys retrieval produces are rendered, because a status field could render only as a\nconstant placeholder. So the defect was a stale sentence in the plan, not a gap in the\ndelivered design.\n\nThe plan now describes what shipped: the render is `alias -- title (similarity <score>)`,\nwith the two citations above for why `score` is the only per-candidate signal available\nand a note that identity falls back to `id`.\n\nThat edit pushed the description to 8,431 characters, over the 8,000-character\nplan-review admission cap, so the prose was compressed back to 7,977 without dropping a\nfact or a citation -- shortened path prefixes already unambiguous in context, merged\nredundant clauses, and removed one sentence ruling out `coverage_extra` as the recording\nseam. The plan still rules out the two alternatives that matter: a second retrieval path,\nand a separate pass rather than the existing extra-context seam.\n\nThe second finding in that run, `project.net-mechanism`, was dropped by the reviewer, not\nblocking.\n\nPlan review is re-running against the merged tree."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793721624195578783,"uuid":"6fb5c058-2eae-4997-8c5e-22b3a867f3e8"}